HST Office on Windows: no legacy CLI-UNO (.NET Framework) bridge assemblies in the MSI.

The Windows build now configures with --disable-cli, so cli_ure, cli_basetypes, cli_uretypes,
cli_cppuhelper and cli_oootypes are no longer built. scp2 still listed them, together with their
publisher policy assemblies and .config files, as ASSEMBLY files, which the installer writes into
the MsiAssembly tables and installs into the .NET Global Assembly Cache. Installing them failed on
some machines (error 1304 writing cli_uretypes.dll; Smart App Control blocking the unsigned
policy.1.0.cli_cppuhelper.dll at the GAC step) and only .NET programs that drive the office
through the old CLI binding use them. Basic, VBA, the OLE automation bridge (HSTOffice.* COM
names), Python and HST's extensions do not.

The scp2 items are now built only when ENABLE_CLI is set (it is defined for scp2/ure and
scp2/ooo), so a --disable-cli build packages none of them. The module file lists keep the item
names; an undefined name there is ignored, as on Windows Arm64. An upgrade from HST Office 1.0.0
or 1.0.1 removes the old product, its GAC assemblies included, in RemoveExistingProducts
(sequence 675, before InstallInitialize).

License: modified upstream files keep their license (MPL-2.0).

--- a/scp2/InstallModule_ooo.mk
+++ b/scp2/InstallModule_ooo.mk
@@ -21,6 +21,7 @@
 ))
 
 $(eval $(call gb_InstallModule_define_if_set,scp2/ooo,\
+	ENABLE_CLI \
 	ENABLE_MACOSX_SANDBOX \
 	ENABLE_ONLINE_UPDATE \
 	MPL_SUBSET \
--- a/scp2/InstallModule_ure.mk
+++ b/scp2/InstallModule_ure.mk
@@ -12,6 +12,7 @@
 $(eval $(call gb_InstallModule_use_auto_install_libs,scp2/ure,ure))
 
 $(eval $(call gb_InstallModule_define_if_set,scp2/ure,\
+	ENABLE_CLI \
 	ENABLE_MACOSX_SANDBOX \
 ))
 
--- a/scp2/source/ooo/file_library_ooo.scp
+++ b/scp2/source/ooo/file_library_ooo.scp
@@ -26,7 +26,7 @@
